==Old English==

===Etymology===
From {{inh|ang|gem-pro|*tīmô}}, from {{etyl|ine-pro|ang}} {{m|ine-pro|*tī-}}, from {{m|ine-pro|*dī-||time}}. Cognate with Old Norse {{m|non|tími}} (Danish {{m|da|time}}, Swedish {{m|sv|timme}}). Related to Old English {{m|ang|tīd}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/ˈtiːma/|lang=ang}}

===Noun===
{{ang-noun|head=tīma|m|tīman}}

# a [[time]]
#* '''c. 995''', [[w:Ælfric of Eynsham|Ælfric]], [https://archive.org/stream/grammatik00aelfuoft#page/n143 ''Extracts on Grammar in English'']
#*: ''Hit is '''tīma''' tō eriġenne.''
#*:: It's '''time''' to plough.
#* '''c. 897''', {{w|Alfred the Great}}, [https://books.google.com/books?id=Y3lMAAAAcAAJ&pg=PA459 translation] of [[w:Pope Gregory I|Pope Gregory's]] ''{{w|Pastoral Care}}''
#*: ''Nū ūs is '''tīma''' þæt wē onwæcnen of slǣpe.''
#*:: Now it is '''time''' for us to awake from sleep.

